| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| Sword            | Balanced combos, counters, adaptable combat              |
| Greatsword       | Heavy commitment, armour breaking, devastating finishers |
| Dual blades      | Speed, evasion, continuous pressure                      |
| Spear            | Range, precision, thrusts and counterattacks             |
| Dagger           | Mobility, weak-point attacks, critical strikes           |
| Bow              | Precision, positioning, elemental ammunition             |
| Greatbow         | Heavy ranged attacks, piercing shots                     |
| Staff            | Spellcasting, elemental manipulation                     |
| Grimoire         | Spell combinations, battlefield control                  |
| Gauntlets        | Close-range combos, impact and grappling                 |
| Scythe           | Wide attacks, risk-reward mechanics                      |
| Shield and sword | Defence, counters, protection and aggression             |

I'd also investigate weapon-specific mechanics rather than merely assigning different animations to the same underlying attacks.

Additional combat mechanics worth exploring:

* Weapon proficiency trees.

* Weapon-specific ultimate techniques.

* Perfect dodge follow-ups.

* Guard counters.

* Directional attacks.

* Aerial combat and launchers.

* Environmental combat interactions.

* Breakable armour and monster parts.

* Elemental combinations.

* Status-effect interactions.

* Enemy resistance adaptation.

* Boss-specific mechanics.

* Combat challenges and training grounds.

* Weapon switching combos, if compatible with the intended control scheme.

* Signature attacks unlocked through mastery rather than just levelling.

One particularly important addition: enemy intelligence.

Enemies should not simply have different health pools and damage values.

They should have different tactical personalities:

* Predators stalk and isolate.

* Pack hunters surround.

* Territorial monsters defend specific areas.

* Intelligent humanoids coordinate.

* Magical enemies control distance.

* Armoured creatures punish frontal attacks.

* Ambush predators exploit poor positioning.

* Bosses introduce new tactical problems as the fight progresses.

The goal is to make the player recognise an enemy's behaviour and develop a strategy.

