### 3\. Critical issues to investigate in the existing documentation

I found some concrete areas that deserve attention in the canonical document.

These are not necessarily bugs in the game. They are design or documentation issues that need to be resolved before they propagate into production.

| Issue                           | Why it matters                                                                                                         |
| ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Brindlewick's region assignment | Canon identifies a discrepancy between Whisperwood and Aldmere across region, map and quest data.                      |
| Physical geography              | Several regions have discrepancies between their descriptions and generated geography.                                 |
| Progression scale               | Level 300, Transcendence, many class tiers and a 1,000-hour aspiration need carefully tested pacing.                   |
| Monster content volume          | 6,754 population variants create significant balancing, testing and asset-management demands.                          |
| NPC scale                       | 2,500 named NPCs need a clear distinction between authored identity, simulated behaviour and actual interaction depth. |
| Regional Tyrants                | Forty regional bosses and siege cycles risk becoming repetitive without distinct encounter identities.                 |
| Companion roster                | The proposed roster cap is sensible, but the existing scope still requires substantial writing and testing.            |
| Feature expansion               | The 52 signature features and 30 adapted mechanics need prioritisation rather than automatic adoption.                 |

Source: [Aetheria's Canon.md](https://github.com/marcevm1-del/Aetheria-Game/blob/main/Canon.md?utm_source=chatgpt.com).

### My strongest production recommendation

Do not make the game bigger indiscriminately.

Make its existing systems deeper, more interconnected and more rewarding.

For example, one excellent region with:

* A memorable town.

* Interesting NPCs.

* A functioning economy.

* A convincing monster ecosystem.

* A beautiful environment.

* Several interconnected quests.

* A unique dungeon.

* A compelling boss.

* A visible liberation outcome.

...is worth more than five enormous regions filled with repetitive encounters.

I would establish a quality standard for every region before scaling up the world.

